IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sg-core`
IMPORT `sg-periods`
IMPORT `sgf-s216a`
IMPORT `sgf-s216b`

-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, SSO informal consolidation "version in force from 6/5/2026 to 20/10/2026":
-- the procedure for a statutory derivative action, as a regulative layer over the Boolean encodings in sgf-s216a.l4 (s 216A) and sgf-s216b.l4 (s 216B).
--
-- What is here: the complainant gives the directors 14 days' notice (s 216A(3)(a)); the complainant applies to the Court for permission (s 216A(2));
-- the Court grants or refuses (s 216A(3)), or, where notice is not expedient, makes an interim order pending the notice (s 216A(4));
-- the orders the Court may make in granting permission (s 216A(5)); the three rules of s 216B on the stay or dismissal, the Court's approval and interim costs.
-- The parties are the corpus's own `Party` (sg-core.l4). The gates are the existing section functions, called and not restated.
-- Each DEONTIC is gated: where the gate does not answer JUST TRUE, nothing is owed and the rule is FULFILLED at once.
-- The Court has no time limit anywhere in these sections, so no clock is encoded for it.
-- Every step here is a power (a MAY of the complainant or of the Court) except the bar of s 216B(1), which the text lays on no named party and which states no consequence: there is no duty to do once, so no found period is added (notes: `### Duties left without a clock`).
-- Convention for the procedure units (assumed, not ruled): BREACH is kept only for a duty or a prohibition that the text lays on a named party; a power that lapses unused, a condition that is not met and a route that has closed end FULFILLED, with the reason beside the branch. This unit has no BREACH.
-- s 216A(6) (the application is made in a District Court where the action is in the State Courts) says where the application is made, not when or by whom, so it is not encoded as a step.

DECLARE `derivative action act` IS ONE OF
    `s 216A(3)(a) give the directors of the company notice of the intention to apply to the Court`
    `s 216A(2) apply to the Court for permission to bring or intervene in the action or arbitration`
    `s 216A(2) the Court grants permission`
    `s 216A(3) the Court refuses permission`
    `s 216A(4) the Court makes an interim order pending the notice`
    `s 216A(4) give the directors the notice that the interim order awaits`
    `s 216A(5)(a) the Court orders that the complainant or another person controls the conduct of the action or arbitration`
    `s 216A(5)(b) the Court gives directions for the conduct of the action or arbitration by the person so authorised`
    `s 216A(5)(c) the Court orders the company to pay the complainant's reasonable legal fees and disbursements`
    `s 216B(1) the Court stays or dismisses the application or action by reason only of the members' approval`
    `s 216B(2) the Court approves the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal`
    `s 216B(2) the Court orders a party to give notice to the complainant`
    `s 216B(3) the Court orders the company to pay the complainant interim costs`

DECLARE `s 216A(5) the order in question` IS ONE OF
    `s 216A(5)(a) the order about who controls the conduct of the action or arbitration`
    `s 216A(5)(b) the order giving directions for the conduct of the action or arbitration`
    `s 216A(5)(c) the order about the complainant's legal fees and disbursements`

§ `Section 216A(3)(a): the notice to the directors`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the complainant gives the directors the notice; counting="14 days' notice", read as notice given not less than 14 days before the application (sg-periods P5: the clear-days reading is the other, fork F-216A-3 in sgf-s216a.l4); the 14 days are the earliest day for the application, not a last day for anything, so no clock is encoded for the notice and the next rule tests the day from two DATE inputs
-- FORK: s 216A(3)(a) | the notice is a step the complainant MAY take, with no time limit, because the text makes it a condition of the Court's satisfaction and never a duty | the notice as a duty (MUST) with no deadline
-- src:21807 | (3) No action or arbitration may be brought and no intervention in
-- src:21808 | an action or arbitration may be made under subsection (2) unless the
-- src:21809 | Court is satisfied that —
-- src:21811 | (a) the complainant has given 14 days’ notice to the directors
-- src:21812 | of the company of the complainant’s intention to apply to
-- src:21813 | the Court under subsection (2) if the directors of the
-- src:21814 | company do not bring, diligently prosecute or defend or
-- src:21815 | discontinue the action or arbitration;
@ref s 216A(3)(a) the notice to the directors (src:21807-21815)
GIVEN complainant IS A Party
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `derivative action act`
`s 216A(3)(a) the complainant may give the directors notice of the intention to apply to the Court` MEANS
    PARTY complainant
    MAY `s 216A(3)(a) give the directors of the company notice of the intention to apply to the Court`
    HENCE FULFILLED

§ `Section 216A(2) to (4): the application and the Court's decision`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the complainant gave the directors the notice (where the interim order of subsection (4) is the route, the day the application is made); counting="14 days' notice" as above, so notice on 1 March allows an application from 15 March, which is day 14; the Court's decision and the notice that follows an interim order have no time limit in the text ("silent"); the DATE inputs are the notice day and the application day and must agree with the days of the trace
-- FORK: s 216A(3) | the Court's refusal is an act the Court MAY do, open at every outcome of the application | the text names no refusal, only "unless the Court is satisfied", so the refusal could be left out and the Court's silence read as refusal
-- FORK: s 216A(3) | the Court being satisfied is a necessary condition and not a sufficient one: where it holds the Court may grant or may refuse | the Court must grant where it is satisfied
-- FORK: s 216A(4) | after an interim order the complainant MAY give the notice, with no time limit, because the text says "pending the complainant giving notice as required" and states no duty or time | the notice as a duty with a deadline set by the Court
-- FORK: s 216A(4) | the interim order is made "pending" the notice, so after the notice the application for permission is still undecided and the Court's grant or refusal is still to come; the grant is open only where the complainant is acting in good faith and it appears prima facie to be in the interests of the company (s 216A(3)(b) and (c), src:21820-21824) | the notice ends the rule
-- FORK: s 216A(4) | after the notice the Court's grant is open at once, with no wait of 14 days from the notice, because the module has no input for the day of this later notice | "giving notice as required" read as 14 days' notice, so the grant is open only from the 14th day after the notice
-- FORK: s 216A(3) | where the bar applies and subsection (4) does not, the Court may only refuse | the application lapses with nothing further owed
-- src:21797 | (2) Subject to subsection (3), a complainant may apply to the Court
-- src:21798 | for permission to bring an action or arbitration in the name and on
-- src:21799 | behalf of the company or intervene in an action or arbitration to which
-- src:21800 | the company is a party for the purpose of prosecuting, defending or
-- src:21801 | discontinuing the action or arbitration on behalf of the company.
@ref s 216A(2) the application for permission (src:21797-21801)
-- src:21807 | (3) No action or arbitration may be brought and no intervention in
-- src:21808 | an action or arbitration may be made under subsection (2) unless the
-- src:21809 | Court is satisfied that —
-- src:21820 | (b) the complainant is acting in good faith; and
-- src:21822 | (c) it appears to be prima facie in the interests of the company
-- src:21823 | that the action or arbitration be brought, prosecuted,
-- src:21824 | defended or discontinued.
@ref s 216A(3) what the Court must be satisfied of (src:21807-21824)
-- src:21828 | (4) Where a complainant on an application can establish to the
-- src:21829 | satisfaction of the Court that it is not expedient to give notice as
-- src:21830 | required in subsection (3)(a), the Court may make such interim order
-- src:21831 | as it thinks fit pending the complainant giving notice as required.
@ref s 216A(4) the interim order where notice is not expedient (src:21828-21831)
GIVEN complainant IS A Party
      `the person is a complainant` IS A BOOLEAN
      purpose IS A `s 216A(2) what the application is for`
      `the number of directors of the company` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of directors to whom the complainant gave the notice` IS A NUMBER
      `the notice stated the complainant's intention to apply to the Court under section 216A(2) if the directors do not bring, diligently prosecute or defend or discontinue the action or arbitration` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the date the complainant applies to the Court` IS A DATE
      `the complainant is acting in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appears to be prima facie in the interests of the company that the action or arbitration be brought, prosecuted, defended or discontinued` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the complainant has established to the satisfaction of the Court that it is not expedient to give notice as required in subsection (3)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `derivative action act`
`s 216A(2) the complainant may apply to the Court for permission and the Court decides` MEANS
    IF `s 216A(2) the complainant may apply to the Court for permission` `the person is a complainant` purpose EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY complainant
         MAY `s 216A(2) apply to the Court for permission to bring or intervene in the action or arbitration`
         HENCE (IF `s 216A(3) the bar on bringing the action or arbitration, or making the intervention, applies` TRUE `the number of directors of the company` `the number of directors to whom the complainant gave the notice` `the notice stated the complainant's intention to apply to the Court under section 216A(2) if the directors do not bring, diligently prosecute or defend or discontinue the action or arbitration` `the date the notice was given` `the date the complainant applies to the Court` `the complainant is acting in good faith` `it appears to be prima facie in the interests of the company that the action or arbitration be brought, prosecuted, defended or discontinued` EQUALS JUST FALSE
                THEN ((PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(2) the Court grants permission` HENCE FULFILLED)
                      ROR (PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(3) the Court refuses permission` HENCE FULFILLED))
                ELSE (IF `s 216A(4) the Court may make an interim order pending the complainant giving notice` `the complainant has established to the satisfaction of the Court that it is not expedient to give notice as required in subsection (3)(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
                      THEN ((PARTY Court
                             MAY `s 216A(4) the Court makes an interim order pending the notice`
                             HENCE (PARTY complainant
                                    MAY `s 216A(4) give the directors the notice that the interim order awaits`
                                    HENCE (IF `the complainant is acting in good faith` AND `it appears to be prima facie in the interests of the company that the action or arbitration be brought, prosecuted, defended or discontinued`
                                           THEN ((PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(2) the Court grants permission` HENCE FULFILLED)
                                                 ROR (PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(3) the Court refuses permission` HENCE FULFILLED))
                                           ELSE (PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(3) the Court refuses permission` HENCE FULFILLED))))   -- the order is "pending" the notice (src:21830-21831): the permission is still to be decided after it
                            ROR (PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(3) the Court refuses permission` HENCE FULFILLED))
                      ELSE (PARTY Court MAY `s 216A(3) the Court refuses permission` HENCE FULFILLED)))
    ELSE FULFILLED

§ `Section 216A(5): the orders the Court may make in granting permission`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the Court grants permission; counting=silent (the text puts no time limit on the orders)
-- FORK: s 216A(5) | the orders are a separate DEONTIC, one MAY act for each of (a), (b) and (c), because "In granting permission" ties them to the grant and says nothing of an order that comes after it | the orders as the next step after the grant in the application rule
-- The list is open ("including (but not limited to)"): only the three named orders are encoded, and any other order is outside this rule (what the text leaves open).
-- src:21833 | (5) In granting permission under this section, the Court may make
-- src:21834 | such orders or interim orders as it thinks fit in the interests of justice,
-- src:21835 | including (but not limited to) the following:
-- src:21837 | (a) an order authorising the complainant or any other person to
-- src:21838 | control the conduct of the action or arbitration;
-- src:21840 | (b) an order giving directions for the conduct of the action or
-- src:21841 | arbitration by the person so authorised;
-- src:21843 | (c) an order requiring the company to pay reasonable legal
-- src:21844 | fees and disbursements incurred by the complainant in
-- src:21845 | connection with the action or arbitration.
@ref s 216A(5) the orders the Court may make (src:21833-21845)
GIVEN `the order in question` IS A `s 216A(5) the order in question`
      `the Court is granting permission under section 216A` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a person has been authorised to control the conduct of the action or arbitration` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the legal fees and disbursements are reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the legal fees and disbursements were incurred by the complainant in connection with the action or arbitration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `derivative action act`
`s 216A(5) the Court may make the order in granting permission` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the order in question`
    WHEN `s 216A(5)(a) the order about who controls the conduct of the action or arbitration` THEN
        (IF `s 216A(5)(a) the Court may make an order authorising the complainant or any other person to control the conduct of the action or arbitration` `the Court is granting permission under section 216A` EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN PARTY Court
              MAY `s 216A(5)(a) the Court orders that the complainant or another person controls the conduct of the action or arbitration`
              HENCE FULFILLED
         ELSE FULFILLED)
    WHEN `s 216A(5)(b) the order giving directions for the conduct of the action or arbitration` THEN
        (IF `s 216A(5)(b) the Court may make an order giving directions for the conduct of the action or arbitration by the person so authorised` `the Court is granting permission under section 216A` `a person has been authorised to control the conduct of the action or arbitration` EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN PARTY Court
              MAY `s 216A(5)(b) the Court gives directions for the conduct of the action or arbitration by the person so authorised`
              HENCE FULFILLED
         ELSE FULFILLED)
    WHEN `s 216A(5)(c) the order about the complainant's legal fees and disbursements` THEN
        (IF `s 216A(5)(c) the Court may make an order requiring the company to pay the complainant's legal fees and disbursements` `the Court is granting permission under section 216A` `the legal fees and disbursements are reasonable` `the legal fees and disbursements were incurred by the complainant in connection with the action or arbitration` EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN PARTY Court
              MAY `s 216A(5)(c) the Court orders the company to pay the complainant's reasonable legal fees and disbursements`
              HENCE FULFILLED
         ELSE FULFILLED)

§ `Section 216B(1): no stay or dismissal by reason only of the members' approval`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the application is made or the action is brought or intervened in under section 216A; counting=silent (a standing bar with no time limit)
-- FORK: s 216B(1) | the text is in the passive ("must not be stayed or dismissed") and names no bearer, so the bar is not a duty of anyone, as the bar of s 216B(2) is not; the Court appears in the rule only as the party who would do the act, which L4 needs to name a step | the Court as the bearer of a SHANT, with a BREACH when it stays or dismisses on that ground
-- FORK: s 216B(1) | a stay or dismissal by reason only of the members' approval ends the rule FULFILLED, as a closed route and not a breach: the order is made on a ground the subsection excludes, and the text states no consequence (src:21862-21864) | a BREACH by the Court, which was the first reading of this module
-- "but evidence of approval by the members may be taken into account by the Court" is a liberty with nothing to do and no step, so it is not encoded.
-- src:21862 | 216B.—(1) An application made or an action brought or intervened
-- src:21863 | in under section 216A must not be stayed or dismissed by reason only
-- src:21864 | that it is shown that an alleged breach of a right or duty owed to the
-- src:21865 | company has been or may be approved by the members of the
-- src:21866 | company, but evidence of approval by the members may be taken into
-- src:21867 | account by the Court in making an order under section 216A.
@ref s 216B(1) no stay or dismissal by reason only of the members' approval (src:21862-21867)
GIVEN `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the only reason for the stay or dismissal is that it is shown that an alleged breach of a right or duty owed to the company has been or may be approved by the members of the company` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `derivative action act`
`s 216B(1) the Court must not stay or dismiss by reason only of the members' approval` MEANS
    IF `s 216B(1) the stay or dismissal is barred where the only reason is the members' approval of the alleged breach` `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` `the only reason for the stay or dismissal is that it is shown that an alleged breach of a right or duty owed to the company has been or may be approved by the members of the company` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY Court
         SHANT `s 216B(1) the Court stays or dismisses the application or action by reason only of the members' approval`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST FULFILLED   -- closed route, not a breach: the order is made on a ground the subsection excludes ("must not be stayed or dismissed by reason only that ... approved by the members", src:21862-21865); the text names no bearer and states no consequence
    ELSE FULFILLED

§ `Section 216B(2): the Court's approval of a stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the Court is asked to approve the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal; counting=silent (the text puts no time limit on the approval or on the notice)
-- FORK: s 216B(2) | the bar on a stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal without the Court's approval has no bearer in the text and is not encoded as a duty of anyone; the Court's approval and the notice order are MAY acts of the Court | the party who seeks the stay as the bearer of a SHANT until the Court approves
-- src:21872 | (2) An application made or an action brought or intervened in under
-- src:21873 | section 216A must not be stayed, discontinued, settled or dismissed
-- src:21874 | for want of prosecution without the approval of the Court given upon
-- src:21875 | such terms as the Court thinks fit and, if the Court determines that the
-- src:21876 | interest of any complainant may be substantially affected by such
-- src:21877 | stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal, the Court may order any
-- src:21878 | party to the application or action to give notice to the complainant.
@ref s 216B(2) the Court's approval and the notice to the complainant (src:21872-21878)
GIVEN `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court is giving its approval to the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal for want of prosecution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court has determined that the interest of a complainant may be substantially affected by the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `derivative action act`
`s 216B(2) the Court may approve the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal and order notice to the complainant` MEANS
    IF `s 216B(2) the Court may give its approval upon such terms as it thinks fit` `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` `the Court is giving its approval to the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal for want of prosecution` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY Court
         MAY `s 216B(2) the Court approves the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal`
         HENCE (IF `s 216B(2) the Court may order a party to give notice to the complainant` `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` `the Court has determined that the interest of a complainant may be substantially affected by the stay, discontinuance, settlement or dismissal` EQUALS JUST TRUE
                THEN PARTY Court MAY `s 216B(2) the Court orders a party to give notice to the complainant` HENCE FULFILLED
                ELSE FULFILLED)
    ELSE FULFILLED

§ `Section 216B(3): interim costs`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the application is made or the action is brought or intervened in under section 216A; counting="at any time" (the Court may order the interim costs on any day, so no clock is encoded)
-- src:21880 | (3) In an application made or an action brought or intervened in
-- src:21881 | under section 216A, the Court may at any time order the company to
-- src:21882 | pay to the complainant interim costs, including legal fees and
-- src:21883 | disbursements, but the complainant may be accountable for such
-- src:21884 | interim costs upon final disposition of the application or action.
@ref s 216B(3) interim costs (src:21880-21884)
GIVEN `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `derivative action act`
`s 216B(3) the Court may at any time order the company to pay the complainant interim costs` MEANS
    IF `s 216B(3) the Court may order the company to pay the complainant interim costs` `the application is made, or the action is brought or intervened in, under section 216A` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY Court
         MAY `s 216B(3) the Court orders the company to pay the complainant interim costs`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED
